/* 4.0 content: IGCSE Biology 0610, Topics 17 to 21 */
X4TOPIC("bio",{
17:["Two parents with normal skin colour have a child with albinism. How is that possible?","fig:BH_B1701",[["Health","Sickle cell disease is inherited and common in Zambia; genetic counselling helps families understand the risks."],["Farming","Seed companies cross maize varieties to combine high yield with drought resistance."],["Medicine","Stem cells could one day repair damaged nerves and hearts."]],[4,16,17,18],["What are chromosomes, genes and alleles?","How do cells divide by mitosis and meiosis?","How can we predict the chance that a child inherits a characteristic?"]],
18:["Why did fall armyworm pesticides that worked well in 2017 start failing a few years later?","emo:🐛 🧪 ❓|Same pesticide. Fewer caterpillars killed each year.",[["Farming","Pests and weeds become resistant to chemicals through natural selection."],["Health","Bacteria become resistant to antibiotics in exactly the same way."],["Food","Selective breeding gave us high-yield maize, Boran cattle and fast-growing chickens."]],[15,17,18,20],["What causes variation between individuals?","How are organisms adapted to where they live?","How does natural selection change a population over time?"]],
19:["Lake Kariba’s kapenta feed millions of people. What do the kapenta feed on, and what feeds on that?","emo:☀️ ➜ 🌿 ➜ 🐟 ➜ 🧑|Where does the energy in your kapenta come from?",[["Food","Fishing quotas on Lake Kariba and Lake Tanganyika depend on understanding food webs and populations."],["Farming","Nitrogen-fixing beans and groundnuts improve the soil for the next maize crop."],["Climate","The carbon cycle explains why burning charcoal and fossil fuels raises carbon dioxide levels."]],[6,12,19,20],["How does energy flow through a food chain?","How are carbon and nitrogen recycled?","How do populations grow, and how can we count them?"]],
20:["Zambia loses large areas of forest every year, much of it for charcoal and farmland. What happens to the soil, the rivers and the wildlife?","fig:BI_DEFOR",[["Environment","In February 2025 an acid spill from a mine near Kitwe killed fish along the Kafue River and cut off water to the city."],["Food","Fertilisers and pesticides raise yields but can pollute rivers and kill fish."],["Conservation","Zambia’s national parks, fishing bans and community forests help protect species and livelihoods."]],[19,20,21],["How do farming and food production affect ecosystems?","How do pollution and habitat destruction harm living things?","How can we conserve species and resources?"]],
21:["How can a bacterium be made to produce human insulin?","fig:BJ_GM",[["Medicine","Almost all insulin used by people with diabetes is now made by genetically modified microorganisms."],["Food","Yeast in bread, enzymes in fruit juice and bacteria in yoghurt are all biotechnology."],["Energy","Sugar cane and maize can be fermented to make bioethanol fuel."]],[5,12,17,21],["Why are microorganisms so useful in industry?","How are fermenters used to make products such as penicillin?","What is genetic modification, and is it a good idea?"]]
});
X4ADD("bio",{
"17.1":{c:["Topic 16 Reproduction","At fertilisation, a sperm and an egg each bring half the chromosomes. What exactly is on a chromosome?","1. How many chromosomes are in a human egg cell?<br>2. What is a zygote?<br>3. Predict: why do brothers and sisters look similar but not identical?",3],
h:["Every one of your cells has about 2 metres of DNA, yet your cells are tiny. How is it packed, and what is it for?","fig:BH_B1701","DNA is coiled tightly into chromosomes in the nucleus. A gene is a length of DNA that codes for a protein. Different versions of a gene are called alleles, and they give us different characteristics."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: protein synthesis",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the protein synthesis simulation.","Predict the mRNA made from the DNA strand shown.","Run transcription and translation. Record the amino acids.","Change one base and describe the effect on the protein."],{sim:"proteinsyn"}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: the baby’s sex",10,"Board only",["Draw a genetic diagram for XX × XY on the board.","Q1. What is the chance that a baby is a boy?","Q2. Which parent decides the sex of the baby? Explain.","Q3. A family has three girls. What is the chance the next baby is a boy? Explain."],{ans:["1 in 2 (50%).","The father: sperm carry either an X or a Y chromosome; all eggs carry X.","Still 1 in 2: each fertilisation is independent."]}]],
p:[["What are chromosomes, genes and alleles?","write",2,"Define chromosome, gene and allele in one sentence each."],
 ["How is sex inherited?","calc",2,"Draw a Punnett square for XX × XY and give the ratio of boys to girls."],
 ["How does the DNA base sequence decide the amino acid sequence?","expl",2,"Explain how a sequence of bases codes for a sequence of amino acids. Use ‘triplet’."],
 ["What happens in transcription and translation?","sort",2,"Put in order: DNA unzips, mRNA copy made, mRNA leaves nucleus, ribosome reads mRNA, amino acids joined."],
 ["Why do different cells make different proteins?","think",1,"All your cells have the same genes. Why does a pancreas cell make insulin but a skin cell does not?"],
 ["What are haploid and diploid cells?","comp",1,"Give one example of a haploid and one of a diploid cell in humans, with chromosome numbers."]],
s:["A chromosome is a thread of DNA carrying genes; a gene is a length of DNA that codes for a protein; alleles are alternative forms of a gene.","Females are XX and males are XY; the father’s sperm decides the sex of the child.","The sequence of bases in DNA codes for the sequence of amino acids; mRNA carries the code to ribosomes.","All body cells have the same genes, but each type of cell expresses only some of them.","Body cells are diploid (46 chromosomes); gametes are haploid (23)."]},
"17.2":{c:["Lesson 17.1 Chromosomes and genes","Body cells are diploid; gametes are haploid. How does a cell make a copy of itself, and how does it halve its chromosomes?","1. How many chromosomes in a human skin cell?<br>2. Define allele.<br>3. Predict: why do cuts heal?",3],
h:["A cut on your knee heals in a week. Where do all the new skin cells come from?","fig:BH_B1708","From mitosis. Cells next to the wound copy their chromosomes and divide into two genetically identical cells, again and again, until the gap is filled. Meiosis is a different division that makes gametes with half the chromosomes."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: mitosis and meiosis",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the mitosis simulation and run one division slowly.","Count the chromosomes at the start and in each new cell.","Open the meiosis simulation and repeat.","Make a list of three differences you saw."],{sim:"mitosis"}],
 ["model","Model it: chromosomes with string or pipe cleaners",15,"String or pipe cleaners in two colours; paper plates",["Each group makes two pairs of chromosomes (one colour from each ‘parent’).","Act out mitosis: copy each chromosome, line them up, separate the copies into two cells.","Check: does each new cell have the same chromosomes as the original?","Now act out meiosis: pairs separate, then copies separate, into four cells.","Compare the number of chromosomes in the final cells for each division."]]],
p:[["What is mitosis?","write",1,"Define mitosis and say what is special about the two new cells."],
 ["When does mitosis happen and why is it important?","list",2,"Give three situations where mitosis happens in the body."],
 ["What are stem cells?","expl",2,"Explain what a stem cell is and why stem cells are useful in medicine."],
 ["What is meiosis?","write",2,"Define meiosis and say where it happens in humans."],
 ["How are mitosis and meiosis different?","comp",3,"Make a table comparing mitosis and meiosis: number of divisions, number of cells, chromosome number, identical or varied."]],
s:["Mitosis is nuclear division giving rise to genetically identical cells, used for growth, repair and asexual reproduction.","Before mitosis, each chromosome is copied; the copies separate so each new cell has the full set.","Stem cells are unspecialised cells that can divide by mitosis to produce specialised cells.","Meiosis is reduction division: it produces four haploid, genetically different cells (gametes)."]},
"17.3":{c:["Lesson 17.2 Mitosis and meiosis","Meiosis gives each gamete one allele of each gene. When two gametes join, how do we predict what the child will be like?","1. How many cells are made by meiosis?<br>2. Are gametes haploid or diploid?<br>3. Predict: a parent has one allele for brown eyes and one for blue. How many types of gamete can they make?",3],
h:["Two parents with normal skin pigment have a child with albinism. How?","emo:👨🏾 👩🏾 ➜ 👶🏻|Neither parent shows the characteristic.","Albinism is caused by a recessive allele. Each parent is heterozygous: they carry one recessive allele but show the dominant phenotype. If the child gets the recessive allele from both, the child has albinism. The chance is 1 in 4 for each child."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: monohybrid crosses",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the genetics simulation.","Choose two heterozygous parents. Predict the ratio of phenotypes.","Run 100 offspring and compare with your prediction.","Run 1000. Why is the result closer to 3:1?"],{sim:"genetics"}],
 ["case","Case study: sickle cell in families",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. What are the genotypes of a person with sickle cell disease and a carrier?","Q2. Two carriers plan a family. Draw a genetic diagram and give the chance of a child with sickle cell disease.","Q3. Why is genetic counselling useful?"],{study:"Sickle cell disease is an inherited condition that is common in Zambia. People with two copies of the sickle cell allele have red blood cells that become sickle-shaped, block small blood vessels and cause pain and anaemia. People with one copy are carriers: they are usually healthy but can pass the allele on. Many hospitals now test newborn babies for sickle cell disease.",src:"Based on WHO and Zambian Ministry of Health information on sickle cell disease",ans:["Disease: HbS HbS (homozygous recessive); carrier: HbA HbS (heterozygous).","HbA HbS × HbA HbS: 1 HbA HbA : 2 HbA HbS : 1 HbS HbS, so a 1 in 4 (25%) chance of sickle cell disease.","It helps families understand the risks and plan, and get early treatment for affected children."]}]],
p:[["What do the key words of inheritance mean?","write",2,"Define: genotype, phenotype, homozygous, heterozygous, dominant, recessive."],
 ["How do we set out a genetic diagram?","calc",2,"Write out the full genetic diagram for Tt × tt and give the expected ratio."],
 ["How do we construct and read a Punnett square?","calc",2,"Draw a Punnett square for Bb × Bb. What fraction of offspring are homozygous?"],
 ["How do we read a pedigree diagram?","apply",2,"Use the pedigree on the board. Decide whether the condition is dominant or recessive and give evidence."],
 ["How can a test cross reveal an unknown genotype?","pred",2,"A black guinea pig could be BB or Bb. Explain how a test cross with bb would show which."]],
s:["Genotype is the genetic make-up; phenotype is the observable features.","A dominant allele is expressed in the heterozygote; a recessive allele is only expressed when homozygous.","Genetic diagrams and Punnett squares predict the probability of offspring genotypes.","A test cross with a homozygous recessive shows whether a dominant-phenotype individual is homozygous or heterozygous."]},
"17.4":{c:["Lesson 17.3 Monohybrid inheritance","So far one allele has been dominant. Some alleles share the stage. And some genes sit only on the X chromosome. What changes?","1. What is a heterozygous genotype?<br>2. What ratio comes from Aa × Aa?<br>3. Predict: why are more boys than girls colour blind?",3],
h:["A mother is blood group A and a father is blood group B. Their child is blood group O. Is that possible?","emo:🅰️ 🅱️ ➜ 🅾️|Blood groups A, B and a child who is O.","Yes. If the mother is IAIo and the father is IBIo, each can pass on Io, giving IoIo, group O. IA and IB are codominant, so other children could be AB, A or B."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: codominance and sex linkage",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the genetics simulation and choose the blood group cross.","Predict the possible blood groups of the children.","Switch to colour blindness. Compare sons and daughters.","Explain why the pattern is different for sons."],{sim:"genetics"}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: the blood group puzzle",12,"Board only",["Four babies were born at a busy clinic on the same night and need to be matched to parents using blood groups.","Parents: couple 1 (O and O), couple 2 (AB and O), couple 3 (A and B), couple 4 (B and B).","Babies: O, A, AB, B.","Match each baby to a couple. Show the genotypes that make each match possible."],{ans:["Couple 1 (O × O) → baby O only.","Couple 2 (AB × O) → baby A or B; couple 4 (B × B) → B or O; couple 3 (A × B) → any group.","Matching: couple 1 → O; couple 3 → AB (only couple able to give AB); couple 2 → A; couple 4 → B."]}]],
p:[["What is codominance?","write",1,"Define codominance and give one example."],
 ["How are ABO blood groups inherited?","calc",2,"Give all the genotypes that produce blood group A and blood group O."],
 ["What is a sex-linked characteristic?","write",2,"Define sex-linked and explain why it is usually on the X chromosome."],
 ["How do we predict colour blindness in a family?","calc",3,"Draw a genetic diagram for a carrier mother XᴮXᵇ and a normal father XᴮY. Give the chance of a colour-blind son."]],
s:["In codominance both alleles are expressed in the heterozygote.","ABO blood groups: IA and IB are codominant; Io is recessive to both.","A sex-linked characteristic is controlled by a gene on a sex chromosome (usually X).","Males have only one X, so a single recessive allele on X (for example colour blindness) is expressed."]},
"18.1":{c:["Topic 17 Inheritance","Alleles make individuals different. But two identical twins can still grow to different heights. Why?","1. Define allele.<br>2. Which type of division makes gametes?<br>3. Predict: is your height decided by your genes, your food, or both?",3],
h:["Measure everyone’s height and everyone’s blood group. Why do the graphs look so different?","fig:BH_B1802","Height shows continuous variation: many genes and the environment combine, so there is a range with no clear groups (a bell-shaped curve). Blood group shows discontinuous variation: a few distinct groups decided by genes alone (a bar chart)."],
a:[["data","Data task: the class survey",15,"Tape measure; board",["Measure the height of every learner (or use hand span to save time).","Record the class’s blood groups if known, or tongue rolling (yes or no).","Make a frequency table with height groups of 5 cm.","Plot a histogram for height and a bar chart for tongue rolling.","Q1. Which shows continuous variation? Q2. Which is affected by the environment? Explain."]],
 ["disc","Discussion: nature or nurture?",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: for each characteristic, decide genes, environment or both: eye colour, body mass, language spoken, blood group, scar, height.","Share and agree reasons."]]],
p:[["What is variation?","write",1,"Define variation in your own words."],
 ["How are continuous and discontinuous variation different?","comp",2,"Give two examples of each and the type of graph you would draw."],
 ["What causes variation: genes, environment or both?","sort",2,"Sort these into genetic, environmental or both: height, blood group, accent, mass, freckles."],
 ["What is a mutation?","write",2,"Define gene mutation and name two things that increase the rate of mutation."],
 ["Where does genetic variation come from?","list",2,"List four sources of genetic variation in a population."]],
s:["Variation is the difference between individuals of the same species.","Continuous variation has a range of values (height, mass) and is affected by genes and environment; discontinuous variation has distinct categories (blood group) and is mainly genetic.","A mutation is a random change in the base sequence of DNA; ionising radiation and some chemicals increase the rate.","Genetic variation comes from mutation, meiosis, random mating and random fertilisation."]},
"18.2":{c:["Lesson 18.1 Variation","Variation means some individuals are better suited to their surroundings than others. What makes an organism well suited?","1. Give one example of continuous variation.<br>2. What is a mutation?<br>3. Predict: why does a cactus have spines instead of leaves?",3],
h:["Why does a water lily have huge flat leaves full of air, but an aloe in the Kalahari has thick, waxy, spiny leaves?","fig:BH_B1806","Each is adapted to its habitat. The water lily (a hydrophyte) floats to reach light and has stomata on the top surface. The aloe (a xerophyte) has a thick cuticle, few stomata and water-storing tissue to survive dry conditions."],
a:[["case","Case study: plants of the Zambezi and the Kalahari edge",14,"Board only",["Read the descriptions of two plants on the board.","Q1. Which plant is a hydrophyte and which is a xerophyte?","Q2. For each, choose two features and explain how they help.","Q3. Predict what would happen to each plant if they swapped habitats."],{study:"Plant A grows in the Kafue Flats wetlands. Its leaves float on the water, its stomata are on the upper surface, its stems are full of air spaces and its roots are small. Plant B grows on dry sandy soil in Western Province. Its leaves are thick and small with a thick waxy cuticle, sunken stomata and hairs; it has very long roots and stores water in its stem.",src:"Descriptions based on typical wetland and dryland plants of Zambia",ans:["A is a hydrophyte; B is a xerophyte.","A: floating leaves reach light; air spaces keep it afloat and supply oxygen to roots. B: thick cuticle and sunken stomata reduce water loss; long roots reach deep water.","A would wilt and die (loses too much water); B’s roots would lack oxygen and rot in water."]}],
 ["out","Outdoor: adaptation hunt",12,"Notebook",["Find three plants in the school grounds.","For each, sketch it and note one feature that helps it survive dry season conditions.","Find one animal (insect, bird or lizard) and note one adaptation.","Back in class, share the most surprising adaptation."],{safety:"Do not touch thorny plants or insects that sting."}]],
p:[["What is an adaptive feature and what is fitness?","write",2,"Define adaptive feature and fitness in your own words."],
 ["How do we identify adaptations from data?","apply",2,"Use the data on the board to identify which organism is better adapted to a dry habitat. Give evidence."],
 ["How are hydrophytes adapted to living in water?","list",2,"List three adaptations of a hydrophyte and explain each."],
 ["How are xerophytes adapted to dry places?","list",2,"List four adaptations of a xerophyte and explain how each reduces water loss."]],
s:["An adaptive feature is an inherited feature that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment.","Fitness is the probability of an organism surviving and reproducing in its environment.","Hydrophytes have large air spaces, stomata on the upper surface and small roots.","Xerophytes have thick waxy cuticles, sunken stomata, few or small leaves, hairs and deep or wide roots."]},
"18.3":{c:["Lesson 18.2 Adaptive features","Better-adapted individuals survive and reproduce more. What happens to a population over many generations?","1. Define fitness.<br>2. Give one adaptation of a xerophyte.<br>3. Recall from Topic 15: why do some bacteria survive antibiotics?",3],
h:["Why were most peppered moths in English cities black by 1900, when nearly all had been pale 50 years before?","fig:BH_B1808","Soot from factories blackened the trees. Pale moths stood out and were eaten by birds; dark moths were camouflaged and survived to reproduce. Over many generations the dark allele became common. This is natural selection."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: natural selection",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the natural selection simulation.","Predict which colour will become common on a dark background.","Run 20 generations and record the percentage of each colour.","Change the background and run again. Explain the change."],{sim:"selection"}],
 ["role","Role play: bird and beetle",12,"Squares of coloured paper (green and brown), a green cloth or grassy area",["Scatter 30 green and 30 brown paper ‘beetles’ on grass.","Learners are birds: each has 20 seconds to pick up as many beetles as they can.","Count the survivors of each colour. Each survivor ‘reproduces’: add one more of that colour.","Repeat for three generations.","Explain the change in the population using natural selection."]]],
p:[["How does natural selection happen?","sort",3,"Put in order: variation, competition, survival of the best adapted, reproduction, alleles passed on."],
 ["How does natural selection explain the snail example?","expl",2,"Explain why banded snails are more common in hedges and plain snails in grassland."],
 ["How does the peppered moth show natural selection?","expl",2,"Explain the rise of dark peppered moths using the words variation, predation and allele."],
 ["What is adaptation by natural selection?","write",1,"Define adaptation as a process."],
 ["How do bacteria become resistant to antibiotics?","sort",2,"Write the steps that lead to a population of antibiotic-resistant bacteria."]],
s:["There is variation within populations; organisms produce more offspring than survive; there is competition for resources.","Individuals with better-adapted features are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on their alleles.","Over many generations, the frequency of advantageous alleles increases.","Antibiotic resistance spreads by natural selection when antibiotics are used."]},
"18.4":{c:["Lesson 18.3 Natural selection","Nature selects which individuals breed. Farmers can do the selecting too. What happens then?","1. Name the three things natural selection needs.<br>2. How do resistant bacteria arise?<br>3. Predict: why do modern maize cobs have so many more grains than wild teosinte?",3],
h:["Wild teosinte has a few hard grains. Modern maize has hundreds of soft ones. How did people do this without modern science?","fig:BH_SELBREED","By selective breeding. For thousands of years, farmers kept seed from the plants with the biggest, softest cobs and planted them the next year. Over many generations the useful alleles became common."],
a:[["case","Case study: breeding drought-tolerant maize",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. Which features were the breeders selecting for?","Q2. Describe the steps of selective breeding in this example.","Q3. Why does it take many generations?","Q4. Suggest one disadvantage of every farmer planting the same variety."],{study:"Droughts are becoming more frequent in southern Zambia. Plant breeders working with Zambian seed companies have crossed maize varieties that keep growing during short dry spells with varieties that give high yields. Each season they choose the best plants, cross them again and test the offspring at many sites. After many years, they release new varieties to farmers.",src:"Based on reports from CIMMYT and Zambian seed breeders on drought-tolerant maize",ans:["Drought tolerance and high yield (also disease resistance).","Choose parents with the desired features; cross them; select the best offspring; repeat over many generations.","Each generation only increases the useful alleles a little; one generation takes a season.","Little genetic variation, so a new disease or pest could destroy the whole crop."]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: natural versus artificial selection",8,"None",["Pairs list similarities and differences between natural and artificial selection.","Share: which one is faster, and why?"]]],
p:[["What is selective breeding?","write",1,"Define selective breeding and list the steps."],
 ["How are domesticated animals selectively bred?","list",2,"Give two features breeders select for in cattle or chickens and the reason."],
 ["How are crop plants selectively bred?","list",2,"Give three features breeders select for in maize or cassava."],
 ["How are artificial and natural selection different?","comp",2,"Make a table comparing who or what selects, how fast, and whether the features help survival in the wild."]],
s:["Selective breeding is choosing individuals with desirable features and breeding them over many generations.","Animals are bred for features such as milk yield, meat, docility and disease resistance.","Crops are bred for yield, drought and disease resistance and taste.","In artificial selection humans choose; in natural selection the environment does."]},
"19.1":{c:["Topic 18 Variation and selection","Natural selection depends on organisms competing for food. Where does all the food energy on Earth start?","1. Define natural selection in one sentence.<br>2. Recall from Topic 6: what do plants make by photosynthesis?<br>3. Predict: why are there more mice than snakes in a field?",3],
h:["Why are there millions of kapenta in Lake Kariba but only a few hundred tigerfish of the same total mass… and even fewer fish eagles?","emo:☀️ 🌿 🐟 🐠 🦅|Fewer animals at each step up.","At each step in a food chain much of the energy is lost as heat from respiration and in waste, so less energy reaches the next level. There is less biomass at each trophic level and food chains rarely have more than five links."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: build a food web",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the food web simulation.","Predict what happens to the other species if one is removed.","Remove a producer, then a top predator. Record the changes.","Explain each change by following the arrows."],{sim:"foodweb"}],
 ["out","Outdoor: a schoolyard food chain",15,"Notebook; quadrat (string square) if available",["In pairs, spend 10 minutes looking for producers and consumers in one small area.","Record each organism and what you think it eats.","Build two food chains from your findings.","Back in class, combine chains into a class food web on the board."],{safety:"Watch for ants and bees; do not touch unknown animals."}]],
p:[["Where does the energy in a food chain come from?","think",1,"Explain why the Sun is the source of energy for almost all food chains."],
 ["What is a food chain and what are trophic levels?","label",2,"Write a food chain from Lake Kariba and label each trophic level."],
 ["What is a food web, and what happens if one species is removed?","pred",2,"Predict what happens to a food web if all the kapenta are overfished. Give two effects."],
 ["What does a pyramid of numbers show?","sketch",2,"Sketch a pyramid of numbers for: one mango tree → many caterpillars → a few birds. Why is it not a pyramid shape?"],
 ["What does a pyramid of biomass show?","comp",2,"Explain why a pyramid of biomass is always a pyramid shape for the mango tree food chain."],
 ["What does a pyramid of energy show?","expl",2,"Explain why a pyramid of energy can never be upside down."],
 ["Why is it more efficient for humans to eat plants than animals?","calc",2,"If 10% of energy passes to the next level, how much of 10 000 kJ in maize reaches a person who eats chicken fed on maize?"]],
s:["Energy enters food chains from the Sun through photosynthesis by producers.","A food chain shows the transfer of energy; trophic levels are producers, primary, secondary and tertiary consumers.","Energy is lost at each level (respiration, heat, waste), so food chains are short.","Pyramids of numbers can be inverted; pyramids of biomass and energy usually are not.","Eating crops directly feeds more people per hectare than eating livestock."]},
"19.2":{c:["Lesson 19.1 Food chains and energy flow","Energy flows through a food chain and is lost as heat. Atoms such as carbon and nitrogen are not lost. Where do they go?","1. What is a producer?<br>2. Where does energy go at each step of a food chain?<br>3. Predict: what happens to the carbon in a dead tree?",3],
h:["Why do farmers grow beans or groundnuts one year and maize on the same land the next?","emo:🫘 ➜ 🌽|Beans this year, maize next year.","Beans and groundnuts have nitrogen-fixing bacteria in root nodules. They turn nitrogen gas into compounds the plant uses. When the roots rot, the soil is richer in nitrate, so the next maize crop grows better. Nutrients are recycled through the nitrogen cycle."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the carbon and nitrogen cycles",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the cycles simulation and choose the carbon cycle.","Predict what happens to carbon dioxide in the air if more charcoal is burned.","Switch to the nitrogen cycle. Remove the nitrifying bacteria and watch the nitrate levels.","Explain each change."],{sim:"cycles"}],
 ["cards","Card activity: build the carbon cycle",15,"Printed cards (1 set per group); a large sheet of paper",["Lay out the store cards (air, plants, animals, dead matter, fossil fuels).","Place the process cards on arrows between the stores.","Check each arrow: does carbon really move that way?","Add one human activity that is changing the cycle and explain its effect."],{cards:[["Carbon dioxide in the air","store"],["Plants","store"],["Animals","store"],["Dead organisms and waste","store"],["Fossil fuels","store"],["Photosynthesis","process"],["Respiration","process"],["Feeding","process"],["Decomposition by microorganisms","process"],["Combustion","process"],["Fossilisation","process"]],cats:["Store","Process"]}]],
p:[["Why must nutrients be recycled?","think",1,"Explain why nutrients such as carbon and nitrogen must be recycled, but energy cannot be."],
 ["How does carbon move through the carbon cycle?","sort",3,"Draw the carbon cycle with arrows for photosynthesis, respiration, feeding, decomposition, fossilisation and combustion."],
 ["How does nitrogen move through the nitrogen cycle?","sort",3,"Draw the nitrogen cycle from nitrogen gas to proteins and back."],
 ["What roles do microorganisms play in the nitrogen cycle?","comp",2,"Match each bacterium to its job: nitrogen-fixing, decomposers, nitrifying, denitrifying."]],
s:["Nutrients are recycled; energy flows through and is lost as heat.","Carbon moves by photosynthesis, respiration, feeding, decomposition, fossilisation and combustion.","Nitrogen gas is fixed by bacteria or lightning; decomposers make ammonium; nitrifying bacteria make nitrate; denitrifying bacteria return nitrogen gas.","Plants absorb nitrate to make amino acids and proteins."]},
"19.3":{c:["Lesson 19.2 Nutrient cycles","Nutrients cycle round an ecosystem. But how many organisms can an ecosystem support?","1. Name one process that releases carbon dioxide.<br>2. Which bacteria turn ammonium into nitrate?<br>3. Predict: if 10 rabbits arrive on an island with no predators, what happens to their numbers?",3],
h:["A handful of fish are put into a new fish pond in Chipata. Two years later the pond is full but the number has stopped rising. Why?","fig:BI_SIGMOID","The population grew fast at first (plenty of food and space), then slowed and levelled off. Limiting factors such as food supply, space, predation and disease stop it growing further. This S-shaped curve is the sigmoid population growth curve."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: population growth",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the population simulation.","Predict the shape of the curve for a new population with plenty of food.","Run it. Label the lag, exponential, stationary and death phases.","Reduce the food supply and add predators. Describe the change."],{sim:"population"}],
 ["out","Outdoor: counting with quadrats",15,"A 50 cm square of string or a frame; a tape measure; notebook",["Choose an area of grass. Throw the quadrat randomly 10 times (or use random coordinates).","Count the number of one plant species (for example a common weed) in each quadrat.","Calculate the mean per quadrat.","Estimate the population in the whole area: mean × (area ÷ quadrat area).","Discuss: why must the quadrats be placed randomly?"],{safety:"Throw the quadrat gently and only where no one is standing."}]],
p:[["What are a population, a community and an ecosystem?","write",2,"Define population, community and ecosystem, with a Zambian example of each."],
 ["What controls how a population grows?","list",2,"List four factors that affect the rate of population growth."],
 ["What are the phases of the population growth curve?","label",3,"Sketch the sigmoid curve and label lag, exponential (log), stationary and death phases. Give one reason for each."],
 ["How can we estimate a population using quadrats?","calc",2,"A 1 m² quadrat finds a mean of 4 plants. The field is 300 m². Estimate the population."]],
s:["A population is all the organisms of one species in an area; a community is all the populations; an ecosystem is a community and its environment.","Population growth is affected by food supply, competition, predation and disease.","The sigmoid curve has lag, exponential, stationary and death phases.","Quadrats placed randomly estimate population size: mean per quadrat × total area ÷ quadrat area."]},
"20.1":{c:["Topic 19 Organisms and their environment","Every food chain starts with producers. Humans grow huge areas of just a few producers. What does that do to ecosystems?","1. Draw a food chain with humans in it.<br>2. Why is eating plants more efficient than eating meat?<br>3. Predict: name one problem caused by growing only maize on a farm.",3],
h:["One family farm grows ten crops. A large estate grows only one. Which produces more food, and which is better for wildlife?","fig:BI_PALM","The large monoculture usually produces more of one crop per hectare, using machines, fertilisers and pesticides. But it reduces biodiversity, can increase pests and diseases, and can pollute water. Mixed farming supports more species."],
a:[["case","Case study: the sugar estate",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. List two benefits of the sugar estate to people.","Q2. Why might pests and diseases spread quickly in a monoculture?","Q3. Name two ways fertiliser and pesticide use could affect the Kafue River.","Q4. Suggest one way to reduce the harm."],{study:"Zambia’s largest sugar estate at Mazabuka grows sugar cane over many thousands of hectares, irrigated with water from the Kafue River. It provides thousands of jobs and supplies sugar for Zambia and neighbouring countries. The cane is grown as a monoculture with fertilisers and pesticides to give high yields.",src:"Based on public information about the Nakambala sugar estate, Mazabuka",ans:["Jobs and income; sugar for food and export (also electricity from bagasse).","All plants are the same species and close together, so pests spread easily and have plenty of food.","Fertiliser runoff can cause eutrophication; pesticides can kill fish and insects.","Use buffer strips of natural vegetation by the river; apply fertiliser carefully; use biological pest control."]}],
 ["debate","Debate: should Zambia expand large commercial farms?",12,"None",["Divide the class into two sides.","Each side plans two arguments in 3 minutes, using food security, jobs, biodiversity and pollution.","Each speaker has 1 minute.","Everyone writes their own conclusion."]]],
p:[["How has intensive farming increased food production?","list",2,"List three ways modern technology has increased crop yields."],
 ["What are the effects of intensive livestock farming?","comp",2,"Give two advantages and two disadvantages of keeping animals in large intensive units."],
 ["What are the effects of monocultures?","list",3,"List four negative impacts of large-scale monocultures on the environment."]],
s:["Agricultural machinery, fertilisers, pesticides, herbicides and selective breeding have increased food production.","Intensive livestock farming produces more meat but raises welfare, pollution and disease concerns.","Monocultures reduce biodiversity, increase pests and diseases and can cause pollution."]},
"20.2":{c:["Lesson 20.1 Food supply","Intensive farming feeds more people, but at a cost. What other human activities damage habitats?","1. Name one disadvantage of a monoculture.<br>2. What is biodiversity?<br>3. Predict: what happens to a river if a lot of fertiliser washes into it?",3],
h:["In February 2025, millions of litres of acid mine waste reached the Kafue River. Fish died for over 100 km. What happened to the river ecosystem?","emo:🏭 ➜ 🌊 ➜ 🐟☠️|A tailings dam bursts near Kitwe.","The acid and dissolved heavy metals made the water toxic. Fish and other organisms died, crops by the river were damaged and the water supply to Kitwe was shut off. Pollution destroys habitats and harms people who depend on them."],
a:[["case","Case study: the Kafue River acid spill",15,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. List three living things affected by the spill.","Q2. Why did the government drop lime into the river?","Q3. Why are people downstream in Lusaka also concerned?","Q4. Suggest two ways mines could prevent this happening again."],{study:"On 18 February 2025 a dam holding acidic waste at a copper mine near Kitwe collapsed. About 50 million litres of waste containing concentrated acid, dissolved solids and heavy metals flowed into a stream that feeds the Kafue River. Dead fish were found about 100 km downstream, crops along the banks were destroyed and the water supply to Kitwe was cut off. The air force dropped hundreds of tonnes of lime into the river. The Kafue supplies drinking water to about 5 million people.",src:"Associated Press report, ‘A river died overnight in Zambia…’, 15 March 2025",ans:["Fish, river plants and invertebrates, birds, crops (and people).","Lime is a base: it neutralises the acid.","The Kafue supplies drinking water to Lusaka; heavy metals may travel downstream and build up in food chains.","Stronger tailings dams and regular inspections; treat waste before storing it; emergency plans and monitoring."]}],
 ["sim","Simulation: eutrophication",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the eutrophication simulation.","Add fertiliser runoff and predict the algae and oxygen levels.","Run it and record each change in order.","Explain why fish die even though the fertiliser is not toxic."],{sim:"eutrophication"}]],
p:[["Why is biodiversity under pressure from humans?","list",1,"List three human activities that reduce biodiversity."],
 ["Why are habitats destroyed?","list",2,"Give four reasons habitats are destroyed in Zambia."],
 ["What are the effects of deforestation?","expl",2,"Explain how deforestation affects soil, the water cycle and carbon dioxide levels."],
 ["How do untreated sewage and fertiliser pollute water?","think",1,"Name two ways nutrients reach rivers."],
 ["How does eutrophication kill fish?","sort",3,"Put in order: nitrate enters water, algae grow, light blocked, plants die, bacteria decompose, oxygen used up, fish die."],
 ["Why is plastic pollution so harmful?","list",2,"Give two effects of non-biodegradable plastics on land and in water."],
 ["How do methane and carbon dioxide cause climate change?","expl",2,"Explain the enhanced greenhouse effect in three steps."]],
s:["Human activities such as farming, mining, building and logging destroy habitats and reduce biodiversity.","Deforestation causes soil erosion, flooding, loss of habitats and more carbon dioxide in the air.","Sewage and fertiliser cause eutrophication: algal growth, plant death, decomposition by aerobic bacteria and loss of oxygen.","Non-biodegradable plastics harm animals; methane and carbon dioxide increase the greenhouse effect."]},
"20.3":{c:["Lesson 20.2 Habitat destruction and pollution","Humans damage habitats in many ways. What can we do to protect species and use resources without running out?","1. List two causes of habitat destruction.<br>2. What is eutrophication?<br>3. Predict: why are there closed seasons for fishing on Lake Kariba?",3],
h:["Black rhinos had disappeared from Zambia by the late 1990s. Today there are black rhinos in North Luangwa again. How?","emo:🦏|Gone by 1998. Back again today.","They were reintroduced from other countries and are protected by anti-poaching patrols and community support. Conservation uses protected areas, reintroduction, breeding programmes and laws against hunting to rescue endangered species."],
a:[["case","Case study: the black rhino returns",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. What caused the black rhino to disappear from Zambia?","Q2. List three conservation methods used to bring them back.","Q3. Why does involving local communities matter?","Q4. Suggest one risk that could still threaten the rhinos."],{study:"Black rhinos were hunted for their horns until they disappeared from Zambia in the late 1990s. From 2003, rhinos were moved from South Africa to North Luangwa National Park. A protected sanctuary, rangers, monitoring and work with local communities helped them breed. The North Luangwa population is now one of the most important in the region.",src:"Based on reports from the Department of National Parks and Wildlife and Frankfurt Zoological Society",ans:["Poaching for rhino horn.","Reintroduction (translocation), protected areas and anti-poaching patrols, monitoring.","Communities benefit from tourism and protect wildlife instead of poaching.","Poaching, disease, small population with low genetic variation, habitat loss."]}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: save the fish stocks",12,"Board only",["Fishers on a lake report smaller catches and smaller fish every year.","In groups, list three possible causes.","Suggest four rules that could make fishing sustainable.","Explain how each rule helps the fish population recover."]]],
p:[["What is a sustainable resource?","write",1,"Define sustainable resource and give one example."],
 ["What is sustainable development?","think",1,"Explain sustainable development in your own words."],
 ["How can forests be sustained?","list",2,"List three ways to keep forests sustainable while still using wood."],
 ["How can fish stocks be sustained?","list",2,"List three ways to maintain fish stocks."],
 ["Why do species become endangered?","list",2,"List four reasons species become endangered."],
 ["Why should we conserve species?","list",2,"Give three reasons for conserving endangered species."],
 ["How can endangered species be conserved?","list",2,"List four conservation methods."],
 ["Why is a small population at risk?","expl",2,"Explain why a small population has a higher risk of extinction."]],
s:["A sustainable resource is produced as fast as it is used, so it does not run out.","Forests: replanting, education, legal protection; fish stocks: quotas, closed seasons, net size limits, protected areas.","Species become endangered through habitat loss, hunting, pollution, climate change and introduced species.","Conservation: protected habitats, seed banks, captive breeding, reintroduction, education and laws."]},
"21.1":{c:["Topic 20 Human influences on ecosystems","Microorganisms decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients. People also put them to work. How?","1. What do decomposers do?<br>2. Write the word equation for anaerobic respiration in yeast.<br>3. Predict: why does bread rise?",3],
h:["Why do fruit juice factories add an enzyme to crushed mangoes?","emo:🥭 🧃|More juice, and clearer, with the enzyme.","Pectinase breaks down pectin in plant cell walls, so more juice is released and it is clearer. Many industries use enzymes and microorganisms: this is biotechnology."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the fermenter",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the fermenter simulation.","Predict how temperature and stirring affect the yield.","Change one factor at a time and record the yield.","Explain why the fermenter has a cooling jacket."],{sim:"fermenter"}],
 ["data","Data task: washing powder enzymes",12,"Board only",["Use the data on the board: percentage of a stain removed by biological and non-biological powders at 20, 30, 40, 50 and 60 °C.","Q1. At what temperature is the biological powder best?","Q2. Why does it work less well at 60 °C?","Q3. Give one environmental advantage of washing at lower temperatures."],{study:"Percentage of an egg stain removed in 30 minutes. Biological powder: 20 °C 40; 30 °C 65; 40 °C 90; 50 °C 70; 60 °C 25. Non-biological powder: 20 °C 30; 30 °C 35; 40 °C 40; 50 °C 45; 60 °C 50.",src:"Illustrative data based on a common school investigation",ans:["About 40 °C.","The enzymes are denatured, so they no longer digest the stain.","Less electricity or fuel is used to heat the water."]}]],
p:[["Why are bacteria so useful in biotechnology?","list",2,"Give two reasons bacteria are useful in biotechnology and genetic engineering."],
 ["What are plasmids, and what ethical issues do they raise?","think",2,"Explain what a plasmid is and give one ethical issue in using microorganisms."],
 ["How is yeast used to make biofuel?","write",2,"Write the word equation for making bioethanol and name one Zambian crop that could be used."],
 ["How is yeast used to make bread?","expl",1,"Explain why dough rises and why baking stops the yeast."],
 ["How does pectinase help fruit juice production?","pred",2,"Predict whether pectinase would release more juice at 10 °C or 40 °C. Explain."],
 ["How do biological washing powders work?","expl",2,"Explain how protease and lipase remove stains."],
 ["How is lactase used to help people with lactose intolerance?","expl",2,"Explain how lactase makes lactose-free milk."]],
s:["Bacteria reproduce quickly, have plasmids and no ethical concerns over their use in large numbers.","Yeast ferments sugars to make ethanol for biofuel and carbon dioxide to make bread rise.","Pectinase increases juice yield; proteases and lipases in washing powders digest stains.","Lactase breaks lactose into glucose and galactose for lactose-free milk."]},
"21.2":{c:["Lesson 21.1 Biotechnology","Small-scale fermentation makes bread. How is it scaled up to make tonnes of penicillin?","1. Name one enzyme used in industry.<br>2. Which organism makes bread rise?<br>3. Predict: what conditions must be controlled in a large fermenter?",3],
h:["In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed that bacteria would not grow near a blue-green mould. How did that accident lead to factories making penicillin?","emo:🧫 🍞🟢|A mould on an old culture plate.","The mould Penicillium makes penicillin, which kills bacteria. Scientists later learned to grow the mould in huge fermenters, controlling temperature, pH, oxygen and nutrients, and to extract the penicillin. Industrial fermenters also make mycoprotein and enzymes."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: running the fermenter",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the fermenter simulation.","Choose penicillin. Predict when most penicillin is made.","Run it and record yield over time.","Explain why penicillin is made after the main growth phase."],{sim:"fermenter"}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: the failing fermenter",10,"Board only",["A fermenter making mycoprotein produced half the usual yield. The log shows: temperature rose to 45 °C on day 2; the air supply was blocked for 6 hours.","Q1. Explain how each problem reduced the yield.","Q2. Why must the fermenter be sterile?","Q3. Suggest a control system to prevent this."]]],
p:[["How is penicillin made on a large scale?","sort",2,"Put in order the steps for producing penicillin in a fermenter."],
 ["What conditions are controlled in an industrial fermenter?","label",3,"Label the fermenter: stirrer, water jacket, air inlet, probes, nutrient inlet, outlet. Give the job of each."],
 ["What is mycoprotein and how is it made?","list",2,"List two advantages of mycoprotein as a food."]],
s:["Penicillin is made by the fungus Penicillium in large fermenters, then extracted and purified.","Fermenters control temperature, pH, oxygen, nutrients and waste, and are kept sterile.","Mycoprotein is a high-protein, low-fat food made by the fungus Fusarium."]},
"21.3":{c:["Lesson 21.2 Fermenters","Fermenters grow microorganisms that make useful products. What if a microorganism could be given a human gene?","1. Name one product of an industrial fermenter.<br>2. What is a plasmid?<br>3. Predict: how might bacteria make human insulin?",3],
h:["In 2002 Zambia refused genetically modified maize food aid during a food shortage. Why was the decision so hard?","fig:BJ_B2107","GM maize can give high yields and pest resistance, and the aid would have fed hungry people. But there were concerns about health, about GM pollen crossing into local maize and about losing export markets. Genetic modification has benefits and risks that people must weigh."],
a:[["debate","Debate: should Zambia grow GM crops?",15,"None",["Read the background on the board about Zambia’s 2002 decision and the Biosafety Act.","Divide the class into two sides.","Each side plans three arguments in 4 minutes, using biology evidence.","Each speaker has 1 minute.","Each learner writes their own conclusion with one reason."]],
 ["case","Case study: insulin from bacteria",12,"Board only",["Read how insulin was made before and after genetic modification.","Q1. Where did insulin come from before GM bacteria?","Q2. List the steps used to put the insulin gene into bacteria.","Q3. Give two advantages of GM insulin."],{study:"Until the 1980s, people with diabetes injected insulin taken from the pancreases of pigs and cattle. Some people reacted badly to it, and supplies depended on slaughterhouses. Scientists then inserted the human insulin gene into bacteria. The bacteria are grown in fermenters and produce human insulin, which is purified and used worldwide.",src:"Based on the history of recombinant human insulin (first approved 1982)",ans:["From the pancreases of pigs and cattle.","Cut out the human insulin gene with restriction enzymes; cut a plasmid with the same enzyme; join with ligase; insert the plasmid into bacteria; grow them in a fermenter.","Identical to human insulin, so fewer reactions; made in large amounts; no animals needed."]}]],
p:[["What is genetic modification?","write",1,"Define genetic modification."],
 ["What are some examples of genetic modification?","list",2,"Give three examples of GM organisms and the benefit of each."],
 ["How are bacteria modified to make a human protein?","sort",3,"Put in order: isolate gene, cut plasmid, insert gene, join with ligase, put plasmid in bacteria, grow in fermenter."],
 ["Why are bacteria useful for genetic engineering?","list",1,"Give two reasons bacteria are used in genetic engineering."],
 ["What are the advantages and disadvantages of GM crops?","comp",2,"Make a table with three advantages and three disadvantages of GM crops."]],
s:["Genetic modification changes the genetic material of an organism by removing, changing or inserting genes.","Examples: bacteria making human insulin, herbicide-resistant and insect-resistant crops, crops with added vitamins.","Restriction enzymes cut DNA and plasmids leaving sticky ends; DNA ligase joins them.","GM crops can raise yields and reduce pesticide use, but raise concerns about cost, biodiversity and gene transfer."]}
});
